            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for a team, then the French for a birthday."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: le périphérique",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "le périphérique — \"the ring road (round a city)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Le soir, il y a beaucoup de voitures sur le périphérique. In the evening there are a lot of cars on the ring road."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In everyday speech people often say le périph."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "périphérique is built on périphérie 'outer edge', a learned word that is usually traced through Late Latin peripheria to Greek periphéreia 'circumference', from peri 'around' and phérein 'to carry'. EtymDB has no reliable source line for it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
